## The Contour Gap Rule

The Contour Gap Rule is the one measurement that determines whether a band works with a marquise ring.

**The rule:** The gap between the inner edge of your wedding band and the outer edge of your engagement ring shank — measured at the widest point of the marquise tip area — must be less than 0.3mm.

A gap larger than 0.3mm is visible to the naked eye at arm's length. That line makes the pair look like two separate rings on the same finger. More critically, a gap over 0.3mm creates the rotation and debris problems from Mistake 1.

How do you measure this? You cannot do it at home without a jeweler's loupe. But you can apply it practically. Any contour band for marquise rings meets the standard. Just match the metal gauge to your engagement ring's width.

The Contour Gap Rule eliminates the guesswork. If it is a contour band and the width matches your shank, the gap will be under 0.3mm. A straight band fails automatically. A band more than 0.3mm off in width also fails.

This is the fastest way to filter band options. Straight band? Eliminated. Width off by more than 0.3mm? Eliminated.

