## The Prong Clearance Zone

The Prong Clearance Zone is the space between your band's inner edge and the V-prong bases. Both sit on the inner shank of your engagement ring.

**The safe zone: 0.5mm to 1.0mm.**

Below 0.5mm, the band is close enough that metal-on-metal contact happens during daily movement. Over time, that contact works the prong bases loose from the basket. The V-prong tips begin to shift. The stone becomes vulnerable.

Above 1.0mm, the gap is technically safe — but large enough to be visible from the top view. The rings look like they do not belong together.

The 0.5–1.0mm target hits both requirements: safe from prong damage, invisible from the front.

How do you verify clearance before buying? Ask the retailer for the inner shank profile measurement at the V-prong base position. Most quality retailers have this data. If they do not, request a side-profile image of the ring. Measure the V-prong extension with a ruler against the image scale.

Most marquise rings use standard 6-prong baskets (4 side + 2 V-tip prongs). The typical V-prong base extends 0.6mm below the shank surface. You need a contour band with at least 0.6mm of clearance in the inner top edge. Any quality marquise contour band provides this by design.

