## How to Stack a Marquise Wedding Band

[![Marquise diamond milgrain wedding band in 14k yellow gold](/images/marquise-wedding-band-milgrain-yellow-gold.jpg)](https://www.bluenile.com/wedding-rings?stoneShape=marquise&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=Image)

Some marquise owners wear a second wedding band — either as a stack or as an anniversary band.

Stacking with a marquise center creates two-sided pressure on the engagement ring shank. You are now protecting the V-prong base from both directions simultaneously. This requires even more attention to the Prong Clearance Zone.

For a two-band stack, you need:
- Both bands contoured or curved
- Both bands 0.5–1.0mm clearance from the V-prong bases
- Both bands matching the engagement ring's metal type and karatage

A two-band stack around a marquise is striking. The elongated stone floats between two arcs of diamonds. Many owners plan ahead. They buy a slightly wider band on one side to leave room for a second ring.

Planning to add a second band? Buy the first at the lower end of the width range. That keeps the overall stack from looking too wide.

### Vintage vs. Modern Stack Styles

Vintage-style marquise bands feature milgrain edges and detailed metalwork. They pair well with [art deco marquise engagement rings](/art-deco-marquise-engagement-ring) and [vintage marquise rings](/vintage-marquise-diamond-ring).

Modern bands use clean lines, shared pavé settings, and high-polish finishes. They pair best with a [marquise solitaire](/marquise-solitaire-engagement-ring) or [east-west marquise ring](/east-west-marquise-diamond-ring).

Do not mix vintage band with a modern ring unless you are intentionally going for contrast. Contrast can work — but it takes a specific eye to pull it off.

---

## Marquise Wedding Band vs. Anniversary Band

Many couples confuse "wedding band" with "anniversary band." They are different purchases with different sizing considerations.

A **wedding band** is worn daily from the wedding day. It lives next to your engagement ring permanently. Fit, metal matching, and prong clearance all matter.

An **anniversary band** is often added years later on a milestone. It can go on the same finger or a different one. The style may be straight, eternity, or entirely different.

If you are buying an anniversary band for a marquise ring, you have more flexibility. Perfect contouring is less critical if it sits on the other side or on a different finger.

For a [marquise diamond eternity band](/marquise-diamond-eternity-band) worn as an anniversary piece, stone placement becomes the new question. You want the diamonds sitting at the top, not to the side.

