| **Lab-diamond center stone owner** | [Lab Marquise Accent Band, Rose Gold](https://www.bluenile.com/wedding-rings?stoneShape=marquise&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | [$429](https://www.bluenile.com/wedding-rings?stoneShape=marquise&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | Lab accents on a lab center = maximum budget efficiency | Redirect savings toward a higher-quality center stone |

**What this means:** For most people, the $539 alternating band covers everything — contour fit, diamond accents, correct metal, and prong protection. Spend more only if your specific ring has characteristics that require the higher-end options above.

---

## Expert Summary

The marquise wedding band decision comes down to one question. Is the band contoured to sit against a pointed-tip stone?

Every other choice — metal, width, diamond accents — is secondary to that requirement. A straight band creates a gap. It damages the visual appeal and the structural integrity of your center stone's setting.

The Contour Gap Rule and the Prong Clearance Zone are the two measurements that protect your investment. Every band in this guide meets both standards. Any band you consider outside this guide must also meet both.

A marquise diamond is the most visually striking shape in the portfolio. It deserves a wedding band that keeps it that way for decades.

**Farzana Hasan, diamond expert**, [DiamondCritics](/about-farzana/)

